| Denotes changes made as of 08/04/95


                      PCBoard (R) Control Panel for OS/2
                      ==================================


  What is it?  An Overview of PCBCP
  =================================
  The PCBoard Control Panel for OS/2 (PCBCP) is a utility for configuring,
  launching and controlling various nodes running PCBoard for OS/2 (PCB-OS2).

  At its simplest, it is a GUI version of the PCBMoni program included with the
  DOS version of OS/2.  However, it does much more than PCBMoni.  PCBCP makes
  it easy to set up multiple nodes under OS/2 without having to configure a
  bunch of OS/2 "objects", which means you don't have to manage a bunch of
  icons on your desktop or in the startup folder.  Instead, you put PCBCP in
  your startup folder and let PCBCP start your nodes up.

  In addition to being able to launch programs at startup, PCBCP can also bring
  nodes to the foreground simply by double clicking on the node in the status
  window.  You could, therefore, keep all of your nodes minimized at all times
  (PCBCP can start them minimized if you like) and then just click on the node
  if you want to see what it is doing.

  Further, combined with the SIO VMODEM driver (virtual modem driver for
  internet usage), you can configure PCBCP so that it does not start up a node
  until a call comes in.  In other words, instead of having a PCBoard node
  sitting at the call waiting screen waiting for a call, you can have PCBCP
  hook into VMODEM to determine if a call is coming in and start the node to
  handle that call.  Then when the caller logs off, the node will exit.
  PCB-OS2 is optimized to use a minimum of CPU cycles, but this feature means
  that you can have nodes ready to run *without* using any CPU cycles or memory
  until the need arises.

  Finally, PCBCP is able to monitor the status of your nodes and provide you
  with both a visual as well as an audible warning if a node appears to have
  locked up.  Both of these are configurable, as well as the amount of time you
  want to allow PCBCP to wait before deciding that a node needs attention.  In
  addition to warning you of a problem, PCBCP can even act as a "watchdog" and
  automatically shutdown a node that appears to be hung and then restart that
  node (by closing and reopening the window the node is running in).  In this
  manner, you can keep your nodes minimized and you can even be away from your
  system and PCBCP can call your attention to problems if/when they occur, and
  in some cases it can even fix the problem without human intervention.


  What is it?  A Quick List of Features
  =====================================
  - Native OS/2 PM application
  - Edit a node's status (similar to PCBMoni except that as the node status
    changes, the edit screen is updated to reflect the current status)
  - View a node's last update time
  - Send a message to a node
  - Multiple methods for causing one or more nodes to recycle, drop to dos,
    log a user off, clear a node, etc.
  - OS/2 "notebook style" configuration of nodes
  - Set nodes to run on startup
  - Set nodes to run on connect
  - Globally enable/disable the run on startup/run on connect
  - Define the period of time before warning if a node is inactive and set it
    to display visually and/or sound an audible alarm
  - Automatically shut down and restart a hung node
  - Sysop page notification (optionally bring paging window to foreground)
  - Configure to monitor all nodes, or just the nodes in the window
  - Select a font
  - Treat the fonts as fixed versus scalable
  - Set the number of lines on the screen
  - Set the first node on the screen
  - Set the update interval
  - Configure mouse button 1 (MB1) to either switch to or edit a node
  - Save settings (font, window size, update interval, etc)
  - Accelerator keys (hot keys)
  - Optimized screen updates for low cpu usage


  Setting up the PCBoard Control Panel
  ====================================
  Setting up PCBCP itself is fairly trivial, you can create a program icon that
  runs PCBCP.EXE and then pass the location of the USERNET.XXX file as the
  command line parameter.  Or, if you run it from a command prompt, you would
  run it like this:

      PCBCP C:\PCB\MAIN\USERNET.XXX

  PCBCP can be placed in your startup folder so that it runs whenever you boot
  up.  But more importantly, that means that it can launch any of your
  run-on-startup nodes at the same time so that you keep your startup folder
  relatively clean.


  Configuring the PCBoard Nodes
  =============================
  In the File | Node Settings screen, you can configure one or more PCBoard
  nodes.  In the setup screen, you define the path and filename to execute, the
  working directory, any optional parameters, and optionally a port number.  An
  example setup might look like this:

      Path and Filename:     C:\PCB\STARTOS2.CMD
      Working Directory:     C:\PCB
      Optional Parameters:
      Optional Port Number:  2

  With the above settings, PCBCP will execute STARTOS2.CMD (which may be an
  OS/2 batch file you have created).  It will begin its execution in the C:\PCB
  directory, and it will pass port number 2 to the program via a %PCBPORT%
  environment variable.  The node number is always passed to the program via a
  %PCBNODE% environment variable, but the port number is only passed if it is
  non-zero.

  An example of the STARTOS2.CMD file might look like this:

      @echo off
      rem set any common variables here such as dszlog
      set dszlog=pcbdsz.log
      rem set the port and node parameters to override pcboard.dat
      set pcb=/PORT%PCBPORT% /OS /NODE:%PCBNODE%
      rem now run board.cmd
      board.cmd

  An example of the BOARD.CMD might look like this:

      @echo off
      :top
      c:
      rem change to the node subdirectory (i.e. C:\PCB\1 for node 1)
      cd\pcb\%PCBNODE%
      if exist remote.cmd REN remote.cmd remote.sys
      if exist endpcb     DEL endpcb
      rem run PCBoard while sharing a single copy of pcboard.dat
      pcboard2 /file:c:\pcb\pcboard.dat
      if exist remote.cmd CALL remote.cmd
      if exist event.cmd  CALL event.cmd
      if not exist endpcb GOTO top
      :end

  NOTE:  For the above to work, the BOARD.CMD file is going to need to be in
         your path.  For example, if BOARD.CMD is in C:\PCB, you'll want to add
|        C:\PCB to your SET PATH= statement in the CONFIG.SYS file (under OS/2
|        the CONFIG.SYS file holds the PATH statement for OS/2 sessions).

  The combination of just these two CMD files, and the fact that PCBCP passes
  the node and port numbers to the program it runs, means that you can set up
  *all* of your nodes without having to create any more CMD files, and without
  having to create any further PCBOARD.DAT files, etc.  Instead, all of your
  nodes will share a single copy of STARTOS2.CMD, and a single copy of
  BOARD.CMD, and a single copy of pcboard.dat.

  This works because the CMD files will automatically change to the proper node
  subdirectory on startup based on the %PCBNODE% variable.  And each node will
  use the proper comm port based on the port you set in PCBCP.  And all of the
  nodes will share the same PCBOARD.DAT file by using the /FILE:  command line
  parameter on the PCBOARD2 command line.

  What's left is for you to simply COPY the first node settings to each of your
  other nodes!  Once you have one node up and running, you can set up 10 more
  in a matter of seconds by using the COPY_TO button in the File | Node
  Settings screen.  Using COPY_TO you copy an existing setup to a "range" of
  other nodes and then just go to each node and set the appropriate port number
  for each.

  NOTE:  There is additional information on the topic of setting up a shared
  pcboard.dat file found in NEWBOARD.DOC.

  NOTE:  You can speed up the opening of the File | Node Settings window by
  first going into the File | Node Limits window and setting the first and last
  nodes to cover only the range of nodes you will be setting up and monitoring.

  In the "Run Settings" screen, there are two basic settings, one for defining
  the "Session Type", and the other for the "Auto-Run Settings".

  The session type has three choices.  You can have PCBCP run the node in a
  window.  Or you can have it run the node as a full screen session (which it
  automatically forces to the background).  Or you can have it run the node
  in a minimized window.  If set to run minimized, the node will "start out"
  as a minimized window, but you can bring it to the foreground by double
  clicking on the node status in the PCBCP window.

  NOTE:  An idiosyncracy of WARP is that, if PCBoard is running in a minimized
  window, and PCBoard exits to BOARD.CMD to run an event, and the event shells
  out to a DOS program, the DOS program's window will become visible.  What is
  worse is that, upon closing the DOS window, the window in which BOARD.CMD was
  being executed suddenly becomes visible and so you've lost your minimized
  status.  One of our alpha testers reported that, when using 4DOS/4OS2, this
  problem does *not* occur.  So if you have problems with the window remaining
  minimized, you may want to check into using 4DOS/4OS2.

  The Auto-Run settings allow you to configure PCBCP to start a node
  automatically when PCBCP is started, or have the node remain inactive until
  a call comes in (on a VMODEM node).  When set to "Run on Connect", PCBCP will
  hook into VMODEM and wait for VMODEM to tell it that there is an incoming
  call and, when it does, PCBCP then launches a window to handle the incoming
  call and PCBoard exits (and the window closes) when the call is terminated.

  You can switch to any of the other node settings by clicking the node number
  tab on the right side of the screen, or by clicking on the large left and
  right arrows at the bottom of the notebook page to move backward or forward
  in the notebook.


  Utilizing Some of PCBCP's Features
  ==================================

  Fixed Fonts vs. Scalable Fonts
  ------------------------------
  In the Options | Display screen, there is a pair of radio buttons that let
  you decide if you want to use a fixed font or a scalable font.

  With fixed fonts, you size your window and either increase or reduce the
  number of nodes that are visible in the window (as well as increase or
  decrease the right margin of text).

  With the scalable fonts, everything always "fits" on the screen, according to
  the number of nodes you want to view, and PCBCP then adjusts the size of the
  font accordingly, in order to make it fit.  So you can get really small text
  or really huge text, or just right text, just by resizing your window.  You
  never have to adjust the size of the window to get the number of nodes on the
  screen that you want to monitor.

  To use the Scalable Fonts, you must also indicate the number of lines that
  you want to appear on screen.  This is done in the same Options | Display
  dialog.  You can optionally set a different node number to be the "first node
  on the screen" when PCBCP starts up.

  Of the two methods, the fixed font size will always display "faster", but the
  scalable font size may be more convenient and is definitely easier to use
  than having to go to the Font Dialog and pick a "size" from the menu then
  return back to see the results.

  NOTE:  For the scalable font selection to work, you must be using an outline
  font (i.e.  not a bitmap font).  It can be proportional, but does not have to
  be.  If you have selected a bitmap font, and then go into Options | Display
  and select scalable fonts, the PCBCP window will blank out and remain blank
  until you either change it back to a fixed font, or go into Options | Fonts
  and select a different font.  While the Scalable Fonts option is enabled, the
  fonts that appear to be available in the Options | Fonts dialog will be
  shortened to include only outline fonts to aid in determining which fonts you
  can use.  Also, it should be noted that the SIZE field in the Options | Fonts
  dialog is meaningless when using scalable fonts since the actual size of the
  font is determined by scaling it to the window size.

  Editing the Node Status
  -----------------------
  PCBCP includes an edit screen, similar to PCBMoni's edit screen, where you
  can look at and/or modify the status of the user online.

  There are several ways of getting into the Edit Node Status dialog.  One way
  is to highlight a node by pressing mouse button 1, then select Edit off of
  the main menu.  Another way is to highlight the node with mouse button 1,
  then press mouse button 2 to bring up a pop-up menu and select edit from
  there.  Finally, you can configure PCBCP in the Options | Mouse dialog so
  that double- clicking mouse button 1 will either run a node (or switch to it
  if already running), or you can have it jump into the Edit Node Status
  dialog.

  While in the Edit Node Status dialog, you can change the name, city,
  operation, or message text, just by typing into the appropriate field.  If
  you type into the message field, the Status field will automatically change
  to Broadcast Msg so that if you click on OK the message will be sent to the
  user.

  There are also some Quick Action Buttons which let you quickly change the
  node status without having to go thru the Status drop-down menu.  For
  example, to log a user off, you could click on Force Logoff and then select
  OK.  To clear all of the fields on the screen, you could simply press Clear
  Node, etc.

  Finally, there are two Last Update fields in the top right corner of the
  window which shows first when the last update was made to the usernet file,
  and second, how long ago it was in seconds.  The latter provides a means of
  quickly determining how long a transfer has left (based on the time estimate
  in the operation text and the number of seconds since last update), and also
  gives you some idea as to whether the system is functioning properly based on
  how long it has been since the last update.

  NOTE:  See the NOTE in the next section about the difference in the node
  updates that occur in the DOS version versus the OS/2 version of PCBoard.

  NOTE:  In the title bar of the Edit Node Status dialog box, you may see
  something that looks like this:   "Edit Node Status - Node 1 - Pid 1B"
  The "Pid 1B" portion of the line is letting you know the process id.  This
  information appears only if you have started a session via PCBCP.  This
  information may be useful to you when you are using a program such as PM
  Patrol (and others) which allow you to measure CPU usage and correlate it to
  various process id's.

  Enabling Warnings for System Inactivity
  ---------------------------------------
  PCBCP is able to warn you about nodes which "may" be locked up.  The Options
  | Warnings screen allows you to set the length of inactivity (as determined
  due to lack of updates to the usernet file), in minutes, before it decides
  that a node is inactive.  Then you can have it warn you by highlighting, in
  red, any nodes that appear to be inactive.  Or you can have it warn you by
  sounding an alarm.  Or you can have it do both.  If you have it sound the
  alarm, you should also allow it to highlight the nodes so that you don't have
  to go hunting to find which one(s) are inactive.

  There are four warning settings.  The first one, "Number of Minutes of
  Inactivity" governs all normal online activities.  The second one, "Number of
  Minutes for Events" defines how long to allow an event to run before warning
  that it appears to be inactive.  The third one, "Number of Minutes in Doors"
  defines how long a caller may remain in a door before warning that the node
  may be inactive.  And the fourth one, "Number of Minutes for Transfers"
  defines how long a caller can be in a file transfer before warning that the
  node may be inactive.

  The reason for the separate warning limits is that, while an event is
  running, PCBoard is not updating the usernet file.  Likewise, while a caller
  is out in a door the usernet file is not being updated.  So typically these
  settings will be higher than the number of minutes you choose for "Number of
  Minutes of Inactivity".

  For performance reasons, PCBCP will normally only monitor the activity of the
  nodes that are on the screen, since it can save CPU cycles by not having to
  read or process information that you'll never see or care about.

  However, in the Options | Warnings dialog, you can set it to "monitor all
  nodes", not just those that are on the screen.  In this manner, you can be
  warned of any nodes that have been inactive beyond the limit you have set.
  The warning will initially be audible only, since the node isn't on the
  screen, but you can scroll the nodes into view and see it highlighted in red
  if you have that option enabled.

  Even with the "monitor all nodes" option set, you can still reduce the level
  of CPU utilization by going into the File | Node Limits screen and setting
  the first and last nodes that you really want to monitor.  For example, if
  you have a 250 node license, but you only have 25 nodes online (or 25 that
  you care to monitor), you can reduce the amount of effort put into scanning
  for inactive nodes to 1/10th what it would be if you let it default to
  scanning all 250 nodes.

  One thing to be aware of with the alarm.  PCBCP will only sound the alarm
  when it is refreshing the screen.  So if you have set the update interval to
  3 seconds, you will hear the alarm once every 3 seconds.  If you set the
  update interval to 60 seconds, then you will only hear it once every 60
  seconds.

  NOTE:  The OS/2 version of PCBoard has a separate thread for handling the
  usernet file.  Therefore, as long as PCB-OS2 is running, it doesn't matter if
  the caller has shelled out to a door or other application, it will continue
  to update the usernet file.  The DOS version, on the other hand, will update
  the usernet file only when PCBoard has control.  This can be seen as both
  good and bad...  On the one hand, it means that being out in a door for 30
  minutes under PCB-OS2 won't give you the impression that the system is locked
  up due to the last update having occurred 30 minutes ago, so you'll see fewer
  false alarms amount lockups.  On the other hand, if a door manages to get
  stuck (without actually locking up the machine), it could appear as if the
  node is still active even though the caller has been in the door for a long
  period of time.

  Also, a slight difference in the usernet file format has been made as
  compared to previous versions of PCBoard (both the DOS and OS/2 versions
  released at this time share the same format, the difference is only between
  the newer release and v15.21 and prior releases).  This difference is a
  matter of how the "last update" information is stored in the usernet file.
  It is still a long integer.  But it no longer reflects just the number of
  seconds since midnight.  It is both the date of the last update, as well as
  the number of seconds since midnight (divided by 2).  This allows PCBCP to
  see exactly how long ago the last update occurred, even if it crosses
  midnight one or more times.

  NOTE:  Even if PCBCP is minimized, if you have any warnings enabled, it will
  still monitor the nodes and sound the alarms.  If no warnings are enabled,
  then when you minimize PCBCP it puts itself to sleep to reduce the cpu usage
  down to nothing since the only thing you'd be using it for would be to
  "watch" the status screen and, with it minimized, you can't see it anyway.

  Restarting Inactive Nodes
  -------------------------
  In combination with the Warning Options, PCBCP can act as a "watchdog" for
  your system and can shut down and restart nodes that appear to be inactive.
  It *only* does this if PCBCP has the window open (i.e. the node that is to
  be restarted is currently running and is under PCBCP's control), and only
  after the warning time period, plus the "Minutes After Warning" have
  elapsed.

  For example, you can set the "Number of Minutes for Doors" warning time to
  30 minutes, and then the "Minutes After Warning" to 5 minutes.  With these
  settings in place, after 30 minutes you will begin to hear an alarm
  indicating that there is a problem with the node.  Then, if you are not
  around to take care of the problem within 5 minutes if the starting of the
  alarm, PCBCP will automatically close the window and restart it.

  In effect, PCBCP tries to fix a node that may be "hung" whether it is in
  PCBoard, out in a transfer protocol, running a door, an event, etc.

  NOTE:  PCBCP does *not* try to "restart a node which is NOT active".  The
  reference to "inactive node" here can be thought of as a "hung node".  In
  other words, the node is supposed to be up and running, but the usernet file
  has not been updated and so it "appears to be inactive".

  Put another way, PCBCP does NOT try to restart a node which you have
  purposely caused to exit.  It only attempts to restart a node whose window
  currently exists (under PCBCP's control) which PCBCP then has to successfully
  shut down first, and after shutting down the window, it then attempts to
  re-open the window.

  Apply Status Warning Option
  ---------------------------
  When you are changing the node status, normally PCBCP will warn you with a
  pop-up window asking you to confirm the status change.  This is to prevent
  accidental changes, however, you can disable this warning by removing the
  checkmark from the "Verify Status Changes" option.

  PCBoard Page Alarm Options
  --------------------------
  When a caller is paging the sysop, normally you'd hear the page because
  PCBoard itself makes a beeping sound during the page.  If you are at a
  remote location (in another room, for example) but are running PCBCP to
  monitor the system, you can enable the Page Alarm which will then sound an
  alarm whenever someone pages the sysop.  If you are running PCBCP on the
  same machine where the nodes are located, then it is best to leave the
  page alarm disabled to avoid hearing both alarms at the same time.

  The "Bring Node to Foreground" option is only useful if PCBCP is controlling
  the node.  If it is, then when a caller pages you, you can have PCBCP
  automatically bring the node to the foreground so that you can jump into
  chat with the caller.

  Controlling Nodes
  -----------------
  PCBCP is called a "Control Panel" ...  so what can it do besides run the
  nodes to earn it such a name?  Lots of things and it many different ways.
  You'll want to find which methods work best for you.

  PCBCP can operate on just a single node, or it can operate on a selection of
  nodes which you've highlighted by dragging mouse button 1, or it can operate
  on an entire screen full of nodes, or it can operate on "all" nodes.

  You can apply the various actions "Drop to DOS Now", "Drop to DOS at Logoff",
  "Log User Off", "Recycle Node", or "Clear Node" by going to the Actions menu
  and selection "All Nodes on Screen", or "All Nodes" and apply the action to
  one of those two groups.  Or, you can highlight one or more nodes using mouse
  button 1 and then press mouse button 2 for a pop-up menu to apply the action.

  In this manner, you can drop all of your nodes to DOS (without booting anyone
  off) with just 4 mouse clicks:  1) Select Actions from the main menu, 2)
  Select All Nodes, 3) Select Drop to DOS at Logoff, 4) Confirm the action by
  selecting OK.  Similarly, you could cause all of your nodes to recycle, etc.

  Another item on the Actions menu is "Run/Switch-to Nodes".  After
  highlighting one or more nodes with mouse button 1, this option will
  automatically restart any of the highlighted nodes that are inactive (as long
  as you have configured the nodes, but regardless of the "run on startup"
  setting).  Additionally, if any those nodes are currently running, but
  minimized, they will be brought to the desktop where you can see them.

  The "Restart Inactive Nodes" option will scan for all nodes in your
  configuration which have an inactive status which are set to "run on startup"
  and will run them if they are inactive.

  CAUTION:  One action in PCBCP which is not self-evident but inherent in the
  way OS/2 operates, is that if PCBCP is the "owner" of the node (i.e.  PCBCP
  was used to start a node), then upon exiting PCBCP the node will
  automatically be closed.  This will occur even if a caller is online.  So you
  should avoid closing the PCBCP window unless all nodes are down, or you
  purposely want to close any open node windows.  You can, however, minimize
  PCBCP without causing any harm.


  Optimization Tips for PCB-OS2 and PCBCP
  =======================================
  - Set the update interval to a value of 10 or greater.  The higher the
    number, the less frequently PCBCP runs out to disk to read the usernet
    file, and the less frequently it has to update the screen.  PCBCP *is*
    optimized so that if it reads the disk and nothing has changed (or only
    portions of the information has changed) it will avoid updating the screen
    except when necessary.  Of course, if you want to stay on top of things, a
    lower update frequency will obtain quicker response times.

  - Configure PCBCP to monitor only the nodes that you use.  In other words, if
    you have a 100 node version of PCBoard, but you only have 25 nodes up and
    running at this time, then you can optimize PCBCP's cpu and memory usage by
    setting the first and last nodes appropriately to cover only the nodes that
    you typically have up and running.

  - Set the size of the node status window to view only the nodes you want to
    monitor (i.e. don't monitor more nodes than necessary).

  - Select a non-proportional (monospaced) font if the speed of screen redraws
    is important.  Also, the scalable font feature will always be slower to
    update the screen than the fixed font even if you use proportional fonts
    with the fixed font setting.

  - When considering whether or not to use the "run on connect" feature, if
    most most of your callers connect for only a couple of minutes at a time,
    then it may be better to set up a few nodes so that they are always up and
    waiting for a call in order to reduce the times that the executable gets
    reloaded.  In other words, you should use the run-on-connect feature more
    as a means of "adding more capacity when needed" than as a means of "giving
    away capacity that is normally needed".

  - Store the pcboard2.exe file on a fairly fast local hard drive to improve
    launching times when set to run-on-connect.

  - Use a decent size cache (probably 1M or greater if your system has
    sufficient memory - i.e.  don't starve the system needlessly, but use a
    large cache if possible)

